  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    We are moving to Australia as a family in 2026. Target visa: the 186, officially called "Employer Nomination Scheme". This is not a visa you quickly apply for online, it's a multi-stage process that doesn't work without a sponsoring employer. This article is not a manual, it's our logbook: what we've decided, why, what it has cost and where we stand today.
  </p>

  <p>
    A lot has happened since the first entry in April. I entered on the tourist visa on 25 June, Lucy and the kids have been here since late July. And the part of the plan everything depended on is done: a business in the hinterland wants to sponsor me. Right now the nomination is in progress, the first of two applications. This update is written from exactly that point in the process.
  </p>

  <p>
    Quick intro: I'm Christian, 37, a trained chef with seven years of self-employment. Lucy is 31 and finished her Master's in interior architecture this summer. Joris is six, Linnea two. We currently live in the Northern Rivers region of New South Wales, Byron Bay and surrounds.
  </p>

  <!-- What is the 186 -->
  <h2 id="what-is-186">What is the 186 visa?</h2>

  <p>
    The 186 visa is a permanent work visa for which an Australian employer has to nominate you. Official name: "Employer Nomination Scheme (subclass 186)". The Department of Home Affairs lists it in their current visa overview as one of the standard routes to permanent residency when migrating through a job (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>, 2026).
  </p>

  <p>
    What that concretely means: without an employer who nominates you, there is no 186. Without 186, no permanent residency through this route. And the employer has to offer a position on the official occupation list. The list is called the "Core Skills Occupation List", and both Chef (ANZSCO 351311) and Cook (351411) are on it (as of August 2026, <a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/Documents/core-sol.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Core Skills Occupation List, Department of Home Affairs</a>).
  </p>

  <p>
    Important for us: the 186 is directly permanent. No interim visas, no time-limited work permit. Once it's granted, we are permanent residents of Australia. The route to get there, though, is long.
  </p>

  <!-- Streams -->
  <h2 id="streams">The three streams – which one we take</h2>

  <p>
    The 186 visa has three variants, officially called "streams". According to the Department of Home Affairs these are: Direct Entry Stream, Temporary Residence Transition Stream and Labour Agreement Stream (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>, 2026). Which one fits depends on whether you have already worked in Australia.
  </p>

  <h3>Direct Entry Stream</h3>

  <p>
    This is our route. Direct Entry is for people who were not previously in Australia on a 482 work visa. If you come from offshore directly into the 186, this is the stream. Requirements: passed Skill Assessment, demonstrated English, employer nomination.
  </p>

  <h3>Temporary Residence Transition Stream</h3>

  <p>
    For people who have worked at least two years with the same employer on a 482 visa. A slimmer process, because you've already proven yourself in Australia. Not relevant for us, we come directly from outside.
  </p>

  <h3>Labour Agreement Stream</h3>

  <p>
    A special case. Applies when there's an individual agreement between employer and government, usually because the industry has a labour shortage. Doesn't apply to us.
  </p>

  <!-- Strategy -->
  <h2 id="strategy">Our strategy: tourist visa, then 186 onshore</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part we thought about longest. We deliberately decided against the direct route, against lodging the 186 from Germany. The reason: processing times. A 186 process can take more than a year. Sitting and waiting for a year, with the flat cancelled, the restaurant closed and the kids in limbo, that was not an option.
  </p>

  <p>
    So we re-planned. Our route looked like this:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Enter on a free <strong>eVisitor (651)</strong> tourist visa. Available for German passports, applied for online, three months per stay, no work rights. How the application went is in our <a href="/en/australia-tourist-visa-evisitor-651">eVisitor article</a>.</li>
    <li>Find a sponsoring employer on the ground. I had the three months for that.</li>
    <li>Once the sponsor is in place: nomination by the employer, then the 186 lodged onshore, together with our Migration Agent.</li>
    <li>Lodging automatically creates a <strong>Bridging Visa A</strong>. It takes effect once the eVisitor ends and covers the wait until the 186 decision. From then on I am allowed to work.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The downside was obvious: in the tourist months I am not allowed to work. That means the budget has to last, rental car, accommodation, food, all out of savings. The upside, though, was decisive: once the 186 is lodged and the bridging visa kicks in, we are allowed to work and build our life in parallel instead of hanging on from Germany.
  </p>

  <PullQuote lang="en">Instead of hanging on a year from Germany, we build our life in parallel.</PullQuote>

  <p>
    What it feels like to stake everything on this plan is part of the truth: in early June, a good two weeks before the flight, I lay awake until three in the morning for the first time. Not because of the moving boxes. Because of this one equation: tourist visa plus sponsor within three months, or the plan fails. By the next morning the optimism was back. But if you choose this strategy, you should know such nights come with it.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>Our take:</strong> This decision rode on a single assumption: that we would actually find a sponsor in the tourist months. If it hadn't worked out, we would have flown back. We were aware of that, financially and mentally. This isn't a recommendation, it's our deliberate choice. Every family has different reserves, different visa options, a different time horizon. We took this route because we had lived here before, had contacts, and knew what could go wrong.
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    This is how it then went: the four eVisitor applications from 28 May were confirmed seconds after submitting. Entry on 25 June was uneventful, unusually short queues, not a single question. And the sponsor search, the actual core of the plan, took a good five weeks. More on that below.
  </p>

  <!-- Requirements -->
  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements: Skill Assessment, PTE, age</h2>

  <p>
    For the 186 in the Direct Entry Stream, the Department of Home Affairs requires three core conditions: a Skill Assessment in your occupation, demonstrated English and an age below 45 (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>, 2026). Here's where we stand:
  </p>

  <h3>Skill Assessment as a chef – done</h3>

  <p>
    I passed the TRA Skill Assessment in early 2026. As a self-employed applicant it was particularly involved, because we additionally had to get tax assessments, bank statements and profit calculations translated. We've documented the whole process in a separate article: <a href="/en/tra-skill-assessment-chef-australia">Skill Assessment as a Chef in Australia: Our Experience as a Self-Employed Applicant</a>. The full cost breakdown is in there too.
  </p>

  <h3>PTE Academic – Proficient passed</h3>

  <p>
    I sat the PTE Academic in Hamburg on 11 March 2026 and reached Proficient. I narrowly missed Superior, individual skill scores were just below the threshold. Proficient is well above what the 186 asks for, Competent is enough there. We paid 270 € in test fees, plus travel and a bed at my brother's place, so manageable.
  </p>

  <p>
    A retake for Superior stays an option, but not for the 186. It would be a safety net for a parallel EOI (Expression of Interest) on a points-tested visa. Whether we do it, we'll decide once the 186 route is through.
  </p>

  <h3>Age</h3>

  <p>
    The age limit for the 186 Direct Entry is 45, and the cut-off is the day you lodge your application ("on the date you lodge your application", <a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>, 2026). Lucy (31) and I (37) are clearly under. For families emigrating later in life, this is the critical point: the closer you get to 45, the tighter the timeline becomes.
  </p>

