    </div>
  </AufklappBox>

  <p>
    Later come health checks, police clearances and our agent's fee for the lodgement. All moving costs, from flights to bond, are broken down in our <a href="/en/cost-of-moving-to-australia-family-guide">cost guide</a>. Once our 186 bill is complete, we'll add it to both articles.
  </p>

  <!-- Sponsor -->
  <h2 id="sponsor">Finding a sponsor: how it actually went</h2>

  <p>
    From Germany we had tried for months: Facebook groups, job sites like Seek and Indeed, cold emails to restaurants in the region. In hospitality this practically never works. Most places have a staffing gap, but nobody hires a chef they have never seen in their kitchen. It's different only in international corporations with an Australian arm, where HR can start a nomination from afar. In owner-run hospitality? Forget it.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the plan was: fly over, walk in, CV on the counter. Handing over CVs in person counts as engaged in Australia, not old-fashioned. And that is exactly how it went, just faster than we thought.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the first week I went to a German expat meet-up in Byron Bay. The advice there, in essence: walk around, be open, talk to people, hospitality is always hiring, one contact brings the next. From week two I went through the region with my CV, introduced myself, and always said openly where I stood: tourist visa, not allowed to work until later. Several businesses showed interest in sponsoring me anyway.
  </p>

  <p>
    By late July it became concrete: <a href="https://www.threeways.store/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Threeways of the Valley</a>, a café restaurant in the hinterland, wants to sponsor me. Since then the business, our agent and I have been working through the applications together. That the place sits up in the hills is not a compromise for us, it's the plan: this is the region we want to live in.
  </p>

  <figure class="bild-polaroid">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/assets/images/visum-186-hinterland-hoefe.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/assets/images/visum-186-hinterland-hoefe.jpg"
        alt="Drone shot in low sunlight: forested hills of the Byron hinterland with scattered farms between the trees"
        width="1200"
        height="676"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </picture>
    <figcaption>The hills behind the coastline: this is where the business sits, this is where we want to stay.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    And plan B? It existed, as sober as possible: had there been no sponsor in sight by the end of the three months, we would have flown back. No visa hopping with two small kids. We gave that answer often when people asked what happens if it doesn't work out. It never came to that.
  </p>

  <!-- Nomination -->
  <h2 id="nomination">The nomination: what is happening right now</h2>

  <p>
    The 186 consists of two applications, not one. First the employer nominates the position, only then does the employee apply for the visa. We are standing exactly between those two steps: the business is working on its part, the nomination.
  </p>

  <p class="wartemarke" aria-label="Current status of the process">
    <span class="wartemarke__label">186 process · station 8 of 11</span>
    <strong>Nomination in progress</strong>
    <span class="wartemarke__stand">As of: 15.08.2026</span>
  </p>

  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil lang="en">Right now the ball is with the employer. All we can do is supply paperwork and wait.</Randnotiz>

  <p>
    The nomination is the part of the process an applicant barely sees, and it still decides everything. The employer lodges it online through their ImmiAccount and pays for it themselves: a 540 AUD nomination fee plus the Skilling Australians Fund levy, a one-off 3,000 AUD for businesses with an annual turnover under 10 million AUD, otherwise 5,000 AUD (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/employing-and-sponsoring-someone/sponsoring-workers/learn-about-sponsoring/cost-of-sponsoring" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs, Cost of sponsoring</a>, 2026). For a small business that is a real commitment. One more reason we are grateful our sponsor is walking this route with us.
  </p>

  <p>
    The salary is part of the nomination too: the nominated position has to meet at least the Core Skills Income Threshold, since 1 July 2026 that is 79,423 AUD a year (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/employing-and-sponsoring-someone/sponsoring-workers/nominating-a-position/salary-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs, Salary requirements</a>). The threshold used to be called TSMIT and is raised yearly. More than this frame we deliberately don't publish, my salary is above the threshold, and that is the only number that matters for the visa.
  </p>

  <p>
    What surprised us while explaining it to ourselves: the "number" I am waiting for is officially called a Transaction Reference Number (TRN). It comes into existence when the employer lodges the nomination, not only when it is approved. With that TRN I can link my own visa application and don't have to wait for the nomination decision (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186/direct-entry-stream" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs, 186 Direct Entry</a>). If the nomination is approved first, one deadline applies: the visa application has to be lodged within 6 months of the approval. We coordinate both with our agent.
  </p>

  <InlineCta
    titel="How the nomination goes on"
    text="is what we tell first in the newsletter. Every two weeks, real numbers, no fluff."
    buttonLabel="Get updates"
    href="#newsletter"
    lang="en"
    variante="leise"
  />

  <!-- Family -->
  <h2 id="family">Family on the 186: Lucy and the kids as dependants</h2>

  <p>
    A big plus of the 186: family members ride along as dependants on the same application. According to the Department of Home Affairs, spouses or de facto partners and children count as "members of the family unit" (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>, 2026). For us that means: I am the primary applicant, Lucy, Joris and Linnea are on the same application. If it is granted, all four of us become permanent residents at the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    That has practical advantages. Lucy doesn't need to build her own visa route, the kids don't either. The fees apply per person, that's the downside, but a joint process is still faster and cheaper than four separate ones. One rule is worth knowing: for adult family members without proven functional English, the Department charges a second instalment of 4,890 AUD, due only shortly before the grant (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/employer-nomination-scheme-186/direct-entry-stream" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs</a>). Proof works, for example, through an English test or a degree taught in English.
  </p>

  <p>
    What the 186 does <strong>not</strong> bring immediately: free school and Medicare. Until the grant we pay for both ourselves, the details are in the next section. How we plan Joris starting school and Linnea's care is in this article: <a href="/en/moving-to-australia-with-kids-family-guide">Moving to Australia With Kids: Our Honest Preparation</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Bridging -->
  <h2 id="bridging">Bridging visa and Medicare: the time in between</h2>

  <p>
    The months between lodgement and decision are covered by a bridging visa. An onshore application like ours automatically creates a <strong>Bridging Visa A (subclass 010)</strong>. Important to understand: it doesn't start immediately, it takes effect once your current visa ends, in our case the eVisitor (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/bridging-visa-a-010" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Department of Home Affairs, Bridging visa A</a>). Until then the eVisitor rules keep applying, including the work ban.
  </p>

  <p>
    On working on the bridging visa: bridging visas that arise from an application for a permanent visa usually come without a work ban. Binding, though, is only what your own grant letter says, and that is exactly where we will look once we have it. An earlier version of this article said our agent "sets the bridging visa up with work rights". That is not how it works, the rights follow from the rules, not from something the agent applies for. We are correcting that here explicitly.
  </p>

  <p>
    One restriction we only understood late: on an active Bridging Visa A we must not leave Australia, it does not allow re-entry. Anyone wanting to visit Germany during the wait needs a separate Bridging Visa B first. For us that means: between lodgement and decision there may be more than a year in which a spontaneous trip to Germany is off the table. Every family should know that beforehand.
  </p>

  <p>
    And Medicare? Germany is not among the eleven countries with a health care agreement with Australia (<a href="https://www.servicesaustralia.gov.au/reciprocal-health-care-agreements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Services Australia, Reciprocal Health Care Agreements</a>). As an applicant for a permanent visa you can apply for Medicare once three conditions are met at the same time: application lodged, in the country, and your current visa allows you to work (<a href="https://operational.servicesaustralia.gov.au/public/Pages/your-health/011-44020000-01.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Services Australia, Medicare guidelines</a>). For us that concretely means: while the eVisitor runs we stay privately insured, only with the active bridging visa can we apply for Medicare. What our private cover looks like is in the <a href="/en/health-insurance-australia-moving-family">health insurance article</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Timeline -->
  <h2 id="timeline">Timeline: where we stand today</h2>

  <p>
    Eleven stations, seven done, one in progress. We started the process in December 2025 with the first consultation at the Migration Agent. The stack below is our case file to browse, it opens at the station we are currently stuck at:
  </p>

  <KartenDeck karten={stationen} lang="en" startIndex={7} deckLabel="The 186 application as a file stack: eleven stations from first consultation to decision" />

  <p>
    The optimistic path from here: nomination through, application lodged, bridging visa, and a decision somewhere in 2027. The realistic one: everything can shift. The department publishes official guide values, and they read like this: half of all Direct Entry applications are decided within 10 months, 9 in 10 within 14 months, and one in ten takes even longer (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-processing-times/global-visa-processing-times" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Visa Processing Times</a>, queried August 2026). We have prepared ourselves for the possibility that more than a year sits between lodgement and grant.
  </p>

  <!-- Lessons -->
  <h2 id="lessons">What we've learnt so far</h2>

  <Randnotiz ausrichtung="rechts" pfeil lang="en">Without TRA, no 186.</Randnotiz>

  <p>
    Four things we wish we'd known earlier:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Clarify stream choice early</h3>

  <p>
    The difference between Direct Entry and Transition looks small on paper, in the process it's huge. We only really understood this at the first consultation with the agent. Whoever runs off with a wrongly assumed stream, loses months.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Skill Assessment before anything else</h3>

  <p>
    Without TRA, no 186. And the TRA can, depending on the route, take months. We did it through ATTC because that's faster. Details are in our Skill Assessment article (linked above). Anyone who starts with everything else and tacks the Skill Assessment on at the end, loses time.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Don't try to force a sponsor from Germany</h3>

  <p>
    We tried for a long time to push the entire 186 application through from Germany. Are there people for whom that works? Yes, but rarely, and almost only in corporations with an Australian arm. In owner-run hospitality: don't. Saves your nerves.
  </p>

  <h3>4. After the sponsor's yes, a new kind of waiting begins</h3>

  <p>
    The yes feels like the finish line. It is actually the start of the real bureaucratic chain: nomination, visa application, bridging visa, decision. Every station has its own pace, and none of them is in your hands. If you only plan up to the sponsor search, you plan too short, financially and mentally.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <h3>How long does the 186 visa realistically take?</h3>
  <p>
    The Department of Home Affairs publishes guide values for this: half of all Direct Entry applications are currently decided within 10 months, 9 in 10 within 14 months, one in ten takes even longer (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-processing-times/global-visa-processing-times" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Global Visa Processing Times</a>, as of August 2026). On top of that comes the time before lodgement: Skill Assessment, English test and the employer's nomination. For us, roughly eight months sit between the first consultation and the nomination now in progress.
  </p>

  <h3>Can I work on a bridging visa in Australia?</h3>
  <p>
    Lodging the 186 onshore automatically creates a Bridging Visa A. It takes effect once your current visa ends, in our case the eVisitor. Bridging visas arising from an application for a permanent visa usually come without a work ban, but what counts is your own grant letter. On the tourist visa itself the rule stays: no work.
  </p>

  <h3>Can we fly to Germany while we wait?</h3>
  <p>
    Not on an active Bridging Visa A, it does not allow re-entry. Anyone who wants to leave Australia during the 186 wait needs a separate Bridging Visa B first. We plan for that deliberately: between lodgement and decision there may be more than a year without a spontaneous trip to Germany.
  </p>

  <h3>Do I really need a Migration Agent?</h3>
  <p>
    It's not compulsory. But a 186 application with a family is complex: stream choice, document list, deadlines, nomination and visa application have to fit together. Our first consultation with Johannes Kunz (~250 AUD) cleared up more than our own research before it. For a clear single case without a family, a self-application can work. With kids, we'd advise against.
  </p>

  <h3>What does the 186 visa cost in total for a family?</h3>
  <p>
    Since 1 July 2026 the application fees are 12,280 AUD for two adults and two children: 6,140 AUD main applicant, 3,070 AUD partner, 1,535 AUD per child (<a href="https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/visa-pricing-estimator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visa Pricing Estimator, Department of Home Affairs</a>). On top come Skill Assessment (~4,120 AUD), English test, Migration Agent, health checks and police clearances. For adult family members without proven functional English, a second instalment of 4,890 AUD can apply. All in all we still budget a low five-figure euro amount.
  </p>

  <h3>Why not a 482 visa first and the 186 later?</h3>
  <p>
    The 482 (Skills in Demand) is the more common employer sponsorship: temporary, often faster, but not permanent residency. Our Migration Agent recommended the direct 186 from the start, provided the employer agrees, because it is permanent immediately. Many employers prefer offering the 482 because it binds the employee longer. Our sponsor was willing to nominate for the 186 directly, so we skip the interim step.
  </p>

  <h3>Why not a points-tested visa (189 or 190)?</h3>
  <p>
    The points-tested visas are independent of an employer, but require very high point scores, and for cooks the competition is tight. A parallel EOI for 189/190 remains our plan B, especially if I later push the PTE up to Superior. With a sponsor found, the 186 is our main route.
  </p>

